#
# Backing up over a token whose right ignore holds only part of the
# whitespace after it. When the parser backs up over b, b's left ignore goes
# to accum_ignore, and then detach_right_ignore takes the right ignore off a,
# the token under b. Those came before the ignores already accumulated, so
# they must go on the end of the list, not replace it. Here the append comes
# after backing up over a terminal (and once after an unreduce, in the parse
# error run). regignbt2 covers the append after an unreduce.
#
# This needs two lexical regions. After a token, the scanner reads ignores
# in its own region, and they become its right ignore. With one region these
# take all the whitespace up to the next token, and the next token's left
# ignore is empty. Here a's region has no newline in its ignores, so its
# right ignore stops at the newline, and the rest is scanned in b's region
# as b's left ignore. The spaces at the ends of the IN and EXP lines are
# those right ignores, so keep them.
#
# With the comment ignores, a can have a right ignore of two trees, a space
# and a comment, and b a left ignore of three. The two runs with a comment in
# b's left ignore make accum_ignore hold more than one ignore when the append
# runs, so it has to walk to the end of the list. There is also a sequence
# that needs no backing up, and a parse error after backing up, where dropped
# ignores show only as losses.
#

lex
	token a /'a'/
	ignore /[ ]+/
	ignore /'#' [^\n]*/
end

lex
	token b /'b'/
	literal `!
	ignore /[ \n]+/
	ignore /'#' [^\n]*/
end

def flat
	[a] | [b]

# Unless the input ends in !, the sequence fails. The parser backs up to the
# first b and reduces the first a to flat instead.
def start
	[a b a b `!]
|	[flat*]

parse S: start[ stdin ]
if S {
	if match S [flat*]
		print( 'flat: [', S, ']\n' )
	else
		print( 'seq: [', S, ']\n' )
}
else
	print( 'error: ', error, '\n' )
##### IN #####
a  
  b a b
##### EXP #####
flat: [a  
  b a b
]
##### IN #####
a    


   b a b
##### EXP #####
flat: [a    


   b a b
]
##### IN #####
a 
 b a 
 b
##### EXP #####
flat: [a 
 b a 
 b
]
##### IN #####
a # c
  b a b
##### EXP #####
flat: [a # c
  b a b
]
##### IN #####
a #c  
 b a #d
 b
##### EXP #####
flat: [a #c  
 b a #d
 b
]
##### IN #####
a  # c
  b a b !
##### EXP #####
seq: [a  # c
  b a b !
]
##### IN #####
a  
  b ! a
##### EXP #####
error: <stdin>:2:5: parse error
##### IN #####
a  
 #x
  b a b
##### EXP #####
flat: [a  
 #x
  b a b
]
##### IN #####
a # c
#d
 b a b
##### EXP #####
flat: [a # c
#d
 b a b
]
